Cold-related illness
Social & Environmental
Core temperature <35C, Uncontrollable shivering/cessation, Osborn (J) waves on ECG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-033
Reason for Visit: Acute physiological decompensation and altered mental status following prolonged exposure to freezing temperatures.
Location: Private residence (recently found wandering outside)
Present at Visit: Patient, police officer (first responder)
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly patient found wandering outside in freezing rain, now presenting with profound confusion, slurred speech, and lethargy. Urgent assessment was required to diagnose severe hypothermia, initiate safe rewarming, and prevent fatal cardiac arrhythmias prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I... I can't feel my fingers, everything is so cold... I just want to sleep." (Slurred and slow speech).
History of Present Illness: The police report finding the patient outside without a coat for an unknown duration. The patient exhibits a progressive history of violent, uncontrollable shivering which has now noticeably transitioned to a complete cessation of shivering as the hypothermia worsened over the last hour. Key symptoms include profound confusion, slurred speech, and progressive lethargy. The patient's skin is cold, pale, and cyanotic. The patient reports severe numbness in the hands and feet. The presentation is a direct result of a history of prolonged exposure to freezing temperatures and inadequate winter clothing due to acute dementia-related wandering.
Functional Status: Usually requires supervision; currently acutely incapacitated and semi-comatose
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of fatal ventricular fibrillation, wandering behavior secondary to cognitive decline, environmental exposure

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 | HR: 45 | Temp: 33.2 | RR: 10 | SpO2: 92% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Pale, cyanotic lips and nail beds, apathetic, curled in a fetal position, shivering is absent
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals profound bradycardia with weak, irregular peripheral pulses. Respiratory exam shows dangerously slow and shallow respirations. Neurological exam reveals delayed pupillary responses, slurred speech, and marked lethargy (GCS 11). Extremities are cold and stiff to the touch, but there is no frank waxy skin indicative of deep frostbite. Diagnostic tetrad features are highly suspected/present: core temperature <35C (tympanic measuring 33.2C), complete cessation of shivering, and clinical suspicion of Osborn (J) waves pending hospital ECG.
Environmental Observations: Patient is sitting in a cold room; wet clothing was partially removed by police prior to arrival.
Medication Reconciliation: Unknown at present due to confusion.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cold-related illness (Moderate to Severe Hypothermia) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The global nature of the neurological depression, the profound bradycardia, and the clear environmental exposure mechanism.
2. Narcotic Overdose - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient's age and history of wandering, the presence of cyanosis linked directly to cold exposure rather than pinpoint pupils, and extreme core hypothermia.
3. Hypothyroidism (Myxedema Coma) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, exogenous nature of the cold exposure (found outside in freezing rain) being the primary driver, though baseline hypothyroidism could be an exacerbating factor.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with moderate-to-severe hypothermia (core temp < 33.2C) complicated by a cessation of shivering and profound bradycardia, placing them at imminent risk of spontaneous, fatal ventricular arrhythmias. Deferring this assessment would result in cardiovascular collapse and death. The objective findings of a depressed respiratory drive and severe hypothermia definitively justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to initiate gentle, passive external rewarming and coordinate immediate, gentle transport to an emergency department for active core rewarming.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate removal of all remaining wet clothing. Application of warm, dry blankets and insulation from the floor. Provision of warm, sweet oral fluids (if the patient is conscious enough to swallow safely). Urgent dispatch of an intensive care ambulance.
Positive Behavioral Change: By lying completely still and keeping these dry blankets wrapped tightly around you, you allow your body to trap its remaining heat, which slowly warms your blood and protects your heart from beating out of rhythm.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to geriatric assessment and social work to address the unsafe wandering behavior post-discharge.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Emergency Department resuscitation team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate priority transport to the Emergency Department via ambulance. The patient must be moved extremely gently to prevent triggering ventricular fibrillation. Do not vigorously rub the extremities.

